{% callout type="summary" %}
**Quick answer:** The air in the lungs is divided into four volumes (tidal, inspiratory reserve, expiratory reserve and residual) and capacities made by adding them. Vital capacity is the maximum air expelled after a full inspiration; total lung capacity adds the residual volume. A spirometer records most of them. Residual volume, FRC and TLC cannot be recorded by a simple spirometer.
{% /callout %}

{% cbmeTags codes="PY6.2" phase="Phase 1" /%}

## Why lung volumes matter

Pulmonary function tests (PFTs) measure how well the lungs move air in and out and exchange gases. Most of them are simple, non-invasive and inexpensive. From the physiological point of view they can be grouped by what they study: ventilation, the ventilation-perfusion relationship, diffusion, and pulmonary blood flow and pressure.

Lung volumes and capacities are the starting point for all of this. The total volume of gas in the lungs is divided into compartments called **volumes**, and combinations of two or more volumes are called **capacities**. For many of these, the point of reference is the **end-expiratory level**, the volume of gas left in the lungs at the end of a normal breath.

## The four lung volumes

A PFT tracing includes four lung volumes. Together they add up to the total lung capacity.

{% table %}
- **Volume**
- **Definition**
- **Normal value, adult man**
---
- **Tidal volume (TV)**
- Air inhaled or exhaled with each breath during quiet breathing
- About 500 mL
---
- **Inspiratory reserve volume (IRV)**
- Extra air that can be inhaled by maximum effort after a normal (tidal) inspiration
- About 3000 mL
---
- **Expiratory reserve volume (ERV)**
- Extra air that can be exhaled by maximum effort after a normal (tidal) expiration
- About 1100 mL
---
- **Residual volume (RV)**
- Air remaining in the lungs after a maximum exhalation
- About 1200 mL
{% /table %}

Residual volume can never be breathed out. It is what stays in the lungs even after the deepest possible expiration.

## The capacities

A capacity is the sum of two or more volumes.

{% figure src="/learn-images/physiology/lung-volumes-and-capacities/spirogram-volumes-capacities.webp" alt="Spirogram showing tidal, inspiratory reserve, expiratory reserve and residual volumes with the capacities" caption="A spirogram labels the four lung volumes and the capacities made from them." credit="LungVolume.jpg: The original uploader was Vihsadas at English Wikipedia. derivative work: , CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons" license="CC BY-SA 3.0" source="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lungvolumes.svg" width="960" height="653" /%}

{% table %}
- **Capacity**
- **Made of**
- **Meaning**
- **Normal value, adult man**
---
- **Inspiratory capacity (IC)**
- IRV + TV
- Largest volume that can be inspired from the resting expiratory level
- About 3500 mL
---
- **Functional residual capacity (FRC)**
- RV + ERV
- Air in the lungs at the resting end-expiratory position
- About 2300 mL
---
- **Vital capacity (VC)**
- IRV + TV + ERV
- Maximum air expelled after a maximal inspiration (from TLC down to RV)
- About 4600 mL
---
- **Total lung capacity (TLC)**
- VC + RV
- Air in the lungs after a maximum inspiration; the sum of all the volume compartments
- About 5800 mL
{% /table %}

The **functional residual capacity** is the major reference point for the subdivisions of lung volume. Its position is set by the balance between the inward recoil of the lungs and the outward recoil of the chest wall when the respiratory muscles are at rest.

Two related terms appear in practicals:

- **Inspiratory vital capacity:** the maximum air inspired starting from the residual volume.
- **Closing capacity:** closing volume plus residual volume. It can be expressed as a percentage of TLC.

{% callout type="tip" %}
**Remember the building blocks:** VC = IRV + TV + ERV. TLC = VC + RV. FRC = ERV + RV. IC = TV + IRV. Any capacity that contains RV (FRC, TLC) cannot be measured by simple spirometry.
{% /callout %}

## Normal values in men and women

The values below are averages for a young adult. Women have lower values, and most volumes and capacities are about 20% to 30% less in women than in men.

{% table %}
- **Measure**
- **Men (mL)**
- **Women (mL)**
---
- **Tidal volume**
- 500
- 400
---
- **Inspiratory reserve volume**
- 3000
- 1900
---
- **Expiratory reserve volume**
- 1100
- 700
---
- **Residual volume**
- 1200
- 1100
---
- **Inspiratory capacity**
- 3500
- 2400
---
- **Functional residual capacity**
- 2300
- 1800
---
- **Vital capacity**
- 4600
- 3100
---
- **Total lung capacity**
- 5800
- 4200
{% /table %}

Other useful normal values for an adult male:

- **FEV1:** more than 80 per cent of FVC. The FEV1/FVC ratio is about 0.75 to 0.80.
- **Peak expiratory flow rate (PEFR):** 400 to 600 L/min
- **Resting minute ventilation (RMV):** about 6000 mL
- **Maximum voluntary ventilation (MVV):** 125 to 170 L/min

Several things alter these numbers in healthy people. Values are greater in large and athletic people and smaller in small and asthenic (thin, slightly built) people. They also depend on age, sex, height and, in some references, the altitude at which a person lives. The Indian population shows significantly lower values than its western counterparts, so predicted normograms based on age, sex and height are used. Deviations of up to 20 per cent from the predicted value are commonly seen in normal subjects. Because of this, serial measurements in the same person are very useful: a change of even 5 per cent in an individual is likely to be significant.

## How spirometry records volumes

**Spirometry** studies pulmonary ventilation by recording the movement of air into and out of the lungs. A basic spirometer has a drum inverted over a chamber of water, counterbalanced by a weight. The drum holds a breathing gas, usually air or oxygen, and a tube joins the mouth to the chamber. When the person breathes in and out, the drum rises and falls and a recording is made. The tracing is called a **spirogram**.

A typical practical follows these steps:

1. Fill about three-fourths of the spirometer bell with air or 100 per cent oxygen.
2. Seat the subject comfortably and ask them to relax.
3. Set the recording speed to 60 mm/min.
4. Fit a sterilised mouthpiece between the teeth and lips and connect it to the spirometer.
5. Close the nostrils with a nose clip.
6. Ask the subject to breathe normally through the mouth. This records the **tidal volume**.
7. Ask for a maximal breath in after a normal expiration. This records the **IRV**. Record a few normal breaths afterwards.
8. Ask the subject to breathe out as much as possible after a normal inspiration. This records the **ERV**. Record a few normal breaths afterwards.
9. Ask for a forceful expiration to record the forced vital capacity.

Precautions include: the subject must be comfortable and relaxed, the apparatus must be sterilised and cleaned, the subject should be trained adequately, and the subject should sit with the spine erect. A minimum of three recordings should be taken and the best of three is used, because these tests depend on the effort of the subject.

**What a simple spirometer cannot record:** residual volume, FRC and TLC. Gas left in the lungs after a maximal breath out cannot be seen by a device that only measures air moving in and out. RV is measured indirectly (FRC minus ERV), and FRC is measured by methods such as helium dilution, nitrogen washout or body plethysmography.

A drawback of the gas dilution methods is that regions of the lung that ventilate poorly, for example because of airflow obstruction, receive little of the inert gas and do not take part in the dilution. The result is that these methods often underestimate the true lung volumes.

## Vital capacity: what changes it

Vital capacity correlates well with the ability to take a deep breath and to cough effectively. It is one of the most useful single numbers in the set. Vital capacity depends on chest size, muscle power and body surface area.

{% table %}
- **Factor**
- **Effect on vital capacity**
---
- **Posture**
- Greater when standing erect than when sitting or lying
---
- **Age**
- High in young adults, lower in children and old people. With age, compliance of the lungs and chest wall falls, so VC falls.
---
- **Sex**
- Greater in males, because the chest is larger and muscle power is greater
---
- **Physical build**
- Low in obese and very thin people; high in well-built people
---
- **Pregnancy**
- Low, because chest expansion decreases as abdominal size increases
---
- **Physical training**
- Higher in trained athletes than in untrained people, higher still in swimmers and divers
---
- **Population**
- Higher in the western population than in the Indian population
{% /table %}

**Conditions that decrease VC:**

- Chest deformity, such as kyphoscoliosis
- Loss of functioning lung tissue, such as interstitial pulmonary fibrosis or lung resection
- Neuromuscular disease and ventilatory muscle weakness
- Thickened pleura (fibrothorax)
- Loss of distensibility of lung tissue or pleura, such as atelectasis, consolidation and pulmonary oedema
- Mechanical restriction by pleural effusion, pneumothorax, pregnancy, abdominal tumours or large ascites

There is no pathological condition in which VC increases.

Tidal volume also changes in disease: it falls when lung compliance is decreased or when ventilatory muscle strength is reduced.

## Forced tests: FVC, FEV1 and the ratio

The slow volumes above are one half of the picture. The other half is how fast air can be blown out.

- **Forced vital capacity (FVC):** the maximum volume of air forcibly expired after a maximal inspiration. It is a common clinical index of lung function and also gives information about respiratory muscle strength.
- **FEV1 (forced expiratory volume in one second):** the volume exhaled in the first second of the forced manoeuvre, starting from maximum inspiration.
- **FEV1/FVC:** the fraction of the forced vital capacity blown out in the first second, usually given as a percentage. The normal ratio is about 0.75 to 0.80.

FEV1 is the single most useful test to detect generalised airway obstruction. It is effort dependent, so it must be done properly, and it is relatively non-specific: it points to generalised obstruction, not to small airway obstruction specifically. The **FEV1/FVC ratio** is a more sensitive indicator of airway obstruction than FVC or FEV1 alone. This is why the ratio, not either number by itself, is the key to telling the two main patterns apart.

## Obstructive and restrictive patterns

{% table %}
- **Feature**
- **Obstructive disease**
- **Restrictive disease**
---
- **Examples**
- Bronchial asthma, chronic bronchitis, emphysema
- Pulmonary fibrosis, kyphoscoliosis, fibrothorax, atelectasis, neuromuscular weakness
---
- **Lung volumes**
- Air trapping: abnormally high FRC and RV
- Decreased lung volumes and capacities
---
- **FVC**
- Decreased (air trapping)
- Decreased
---
- **FEV1**
- Decreased
- Decreased
---
- **FEV1/FVC**
- **Decreased**
- **Normal**
{% /table %}

Both patterns reduce FVC and FEV1. The ratio is what separates them: it is **decreased in obstruction and normal in restriction**.

The typical volume pattern in individual diseases shows the same idea. As a percentage of the predicted value:

- **Pulmonary fibrosis** (restriction from increased elastic recoil): TLC, FRC, RV and FVC are all about 60%.
- **Severe emphysema** (obstruction from loss of elastic recoil): TLC rises to 130%, FRC to 220% and RV to 310%, while FVC falls to 60% and FEV1 to about 35%. Loss of the retractive force of the lung increases TLC.
- **Acute asthma** (obstruction from airway narrowing): TLC is normal, RV is raised, FVC is 90%, and FEV1 is about 35% before a bronchodilator, improving to 75% afterwards.

Why TLC changes in these conditions follows from what sets it. TLC is determined by the balance between the force of the inspiratory muscles and the opposing elastic recoil of the lungs and chest wall. Weak inspiratory muscles or stiff lungs reduce TLC. Loss of lung recoil, as in emphysema, increases it. In middle-aged and older people, closure of airways at low lung volumes and the resulting air trapping is an important determinant of RV.

Patients with decreased compliance, such as in pulmonary oedema or fibrosis, must use more respiratory muscle effort to achieve adequate alveolar ventilation, so they are often breathless. Patients with increased airway resistance, as in asthma, chronic bronchitis and emphysema, are also often breathless, and the degree depends on the severity of obstruction.

## Other measurements you should know

- **Inspiratory capacity** is frequently decreased in significant extrathoracic airway obstruction. It is one of the few simple tests that can detect it, because most routine PFTs measure only exhaled flows and volumes, which may be little affected until the obstruction is severe. Changes in IC usually parallel changes in VC.
- **Peak expiratory flow rate (PEFR)** can be measured with a simple portable peak flow meter. It has a mouthpiece, a body piece and a calibrated scale with a marker.
- **Maximum voluntary ventilation (MVV)** is determined by collecting air in a bag as the person breathes as hard and fast as possible.
- **Timed vital capacity and maximum midexpiratory flow rate** are other flow tests in the list of PFTs. The mid-flow measurement is a sensitive indicator of small airway patency.
- **Closing volume** and the **nitrogen washout** method assess the uniformity of ventilation.
- Tests of diffusion, blood gases and pulse oximetry, exercise tests and exhaled nitric oxide complete the wider PFT group.

## Why PFTs are done

Lung volume testing is used to diagnose restriction and to assess hyperinflation and air trapping. Spirometry is used to diagnose obstruction, quantify severity, assess the effects of occupation or the environment, assess the effects of therapy, check preoperative eligibility, and assess disability. Serial tests give valuable information about how disease progresses and how it responds to treatment.

## Mnemonics

{% callout type="tip" %}
**Which capacities need RV?** Think "R for Residual, R for Really can't spirometer it": RV, FRC and TLC all involve residual volume and need helium dilution, nitrogen washout or plethysmography.
{% /callout %}

{% callout type="tip" %}
**Ratio rule for the exam:** FEV1/FVC low means obstruction. FEV1/FVC normal with low volumes means restriction.
{% /callout %}

## Practice MCQs

{% mcq answer="C" explanation="Vital capacity is the sum of tidal volume, inspiratory reserve volume and expiratory reserve volume. Residual volume is not included because it cannot be expelled." %}
Vital capacity is equal to:
- A. TV + IRV + RV
- B. IRV + ERV + RV
- C. TV + IRV + ERV
- D. ERV + RV
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="B" explanation="Residual volume stays in the lungs after a maximal expiration, so it cannot be recorded by moving air in and out of a simple spirometer. FRC and TLC contain RV, so they cannot be recorded either." %}
Which of the following cannot be recorded by a simple spirometer?
- A. Tidal volume
- B. Residual volume
- C. Inspiratory reserve volume
- D. Expiratory reserve volume
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="A" explanation="Functional residual capacity is the sum of residual volume and expiratory reserve volume, the volume at the resting end-expiratory position." %}
Functional residual capacity is the sum of:
- A. Residual volume and expiratory reserve volume
- B. Residual volume and tidal volume
- C. Inspiratory reserve volume and expiratory reserve volume
- D. Tidal volume and expiratory reserve volume
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="D" explanation="FVC and FEV1 are both decreased in restriction and obstruction. The FEV1/FVC ratio is normal in restrictive disease and decreased in obstructive disease, which is why it separates the two." %}
A patient has reduced FVC and FEV1. Which finding best suggests a restrictive rather than an obstructive pattern?
- A. Raised residual volume
- B. Raised FRC
- C. Decreased FEV1/FVC ratio
- D. Normal FEV1/FVC ratio
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="B" explanation="Vital capacity is greater in the standing posture than in the sitting or lying postures. Pregnancy, obesity and ascites all restrict expansion and lower VC." %}
Vital capacity is highest in which of the following situations?
- A. Lying supine
- B. Standing erect
- C. Late pregnancy
- D. An obese person sitting
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="C" explanation="In emphysema the lung loses its retractive force, so total lung capacity increases. Weak inspiratory muscles or stiff lungs reduce TLC." %}
Total lung capacity is increased in which condition?
- A. Pulmonary fibrosis
- B. Myasthenia gravis
- C. Emphysema
- D. Kyphoscoliosis
{% /mcq %}

## Viva questions

{% viva question="Define vital capacity and give its normal value." %}
The maximum volume of air that can be expelled from the lungs after a maximal inspiration, from TLC down to RV. It equals IRV plus TV plus ERV. It is about 4600 mL in men and 3100 mL in women.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="What is the difference between a lung volume and a lung capacity?" %}
A volume is one compartment of the air in the lungs (TV, IRV, ERV, RV). A capacity is a combination of two or more volumes (IC, FRC, VC, TLC).
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Which volumes and capacities cannot be recorded by a simple spirometer, and why?" %}
Residual volume, FRC and TLC. They include the air that remains in the lungs after a maximal expiration, which a spirometer does not see. They are measured by helium dilution, nitrogen washout or body plethysmography.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Name the conditions in which vital capacity decreases." %}
Chest deformity, loss of functioning lung tissue (interstitial fibrosis, resection), neuromuscular disease, thickened pleura, atelectasis, consolidation, pulmonary oedema, pleural effusion, pneumothorax, pregnancy and large ascites.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Why is the FEV1/FVC ratio a better indicator of obstruction than FEV1 or FVC alone?" %}
FVC and FEV1 both fall in obstructive and restrictive disease. The ratio is decreased only in obstruction and stays normal in restriction, and it is a more sensitive indicator of airway obstruction.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Why are PFTs recorded at least three times and the best of three taken?" %}
Because most of the tests are effort dependent. Repeating them and taking the best value reduces the effect of poor effort and gives a reliable result.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="How do the lung volumes of women compare with men?" %}
Most volumes and capacities are about 20% to 30% less in women. They are also greater in large, athletic people than in small, asthenic people.
{% /viva %}

